          %h2#references References
          %p The concept of lagrangian submanifold has been defined/named in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Victor+Maslov"}> Victor Maslov
              ,
              %em Perturbation Theory and Asymptotic Methods
              (MSU Publ., Moscow, 1965; English translation: Mir, Moscow, 1965).
          %p See also:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alan+Weinstein"}> Alan Weinstein
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Symplectic Manifolds and Their Lagrangian Submanifolds
              %strong 6
              (1971) 329346 [
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/0001-8708(71)90020-X"}> doi:10.1016/0001-8708(71)90020-X
              ]
          %p
            An introduction with an eye towards
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
            is for instance in
          %ul#BatesWeinstein
            %li
              Sean Bates,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alan+Weinstein"} Alan Weinstein
              = succeed "," do
                %em Lectures on the geometry of quantization
              %a{:href => "http://www.math.berkeley.edu/~alanw/GofQ.pdf"} pdf
          %p (pages 10 and onward and then section 4.3).
          %p
            Lagrangian submanfolds of symplectic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dg-manifolds"} dg-manifolds
            are called “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Lambda
            \-structures” in
          %ul#Severa
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pavol+%C5%A0evera"}> Pavol Ševera
              ,
